WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.280
- Now that we've talked about
the naturalization process

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:06.710
and the eligibility
requirements for naturalization,

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:09.050
let's talk about how difficult it can be

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:11.830
to become a naturalized citizen.

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:14.710
Immigrant residents seeking naturalization

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:17.260
face barriers throughout the process.

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:20.340
And it's only become
more difficult over time.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:23.330
Immigrants face strict
requirements to become

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:25.260
lawful permanent residents,

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:27.900
and when they try to become U.S. citizens,

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:32.670
they face increased filing
costs and long processing times

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:34.380
for their applications.

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:37.090
Certain groups of immigrants get priority

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:40.590
when they apply for lawful
permanent resident status.

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:43.040
As the Pew Research Center reported,

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:47.180
in 2015, 44% of green
cards went to immigrants

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:50.820
who were immediate
relatives of U.S. citizens.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:54.790
And 20% went to immigrants who
were extended family members

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:58.300
of citizens and lawful
permanent residents.

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:02.700
Having special skills, like
being a talented doctor

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:05.140
or doing innovative research,

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:09.200
or special talents, like
being a gifted athlete,

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:11.910
means you're in the most prioritized group

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:14.560
for employment-based preferences

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:17.130
for lawful permanent residents.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:20.220
If you don't have special
skills or talents,

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:24.390
you can still be eligible for
employment-based residency,

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:27.020
but you have to have an
employer willing to complete

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:30.190
all the necessary paperwork
with the federal government.

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:32.680
As that is an added cost,

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:35.070
some employers refuse to hire people

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:37.140
from outside the United States.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:40.540
So if you don't have
any specialized skills,

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:43.800
a job offer, an employer
willing to file paperwork

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:45.540
for labor certification,

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:48.280
a parent with lawful permanent resident

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:50.070
or citizenship status,

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:53.710
it's difficult to get
lawful permanent status.

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:55.240
The challenges continue

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:57.670
throughout the naturalization process.

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:00.540
It takes years for lawful
permanent residents

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:02.450
to become American citizens.

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:05.730
Remember that even to
apply for naturalization,

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:07.770
lawful permanent residents
have to have lived

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:10.390
in the United States
for at least five years.

00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:13.250
But the process can take
much longer than that.

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:16.300
The median time it takes for
lawful permanent residents

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:19.400
to become citizens is
actually eight years.

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.440
Part of the reason that it takes so long

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:23.960
is because of how long it takes to process

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:26.240
a naturalization application.

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:29.830
The federal government has
experienced a significant backlog

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:32.100
of applications waiting for processing.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:35.630
And the average time to process
a citizenship application

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:38.010
has doubled since 2012.

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:40.720
This is due to a surge in applications

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:44.210
and stricter scrutiny
on those applications.

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:47.460
And the costs associated with filing

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:49.430
a naturalization application

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:51.950
have also increased significantly.

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:53.620
Let's take a look at this chart.

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:58.620
In 1985, it only cost $35 for a person

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:00.560
to file an application.

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:04.370
Now it costs over $1,000 to file.

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:06.514
Why does it cost so much more?

00:03:06.514 --> 00:03:10.910
USCIS argues that the
increased fees are necessary

00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:14.440
to offset the growing cost
of processing applications

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:16.820
and deterring fraud.

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:19.490
And this graph doesn't
cover all of the costs

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:23.470
an immigrant might incur during
the naturalization process,

00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:25.440
since it doesn't include any of the costs

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.680
associated with getting
a lawyer to advise them

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:31.160
or any classes that
they might need to take

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:34.380
in order to learn English or U.S. Civics.

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:38.100
Although immigrant residents
face a number of challenges

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:40.110
to become naturalized citizens,

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:43.970
hundreds of thousands apply
for naturalization every year,

00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:47.510
because the benefits of
becoming a naturalized citizen

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:49.793
often outweigh the costs.

